# Target C-II — the strong-TB1 branch has a linear ordered-support trichotomy

**Date:** 2026-09-17  
**Status:** proof-only high-`t` structural strengthening; no canonical promotion claimed  
**Scope:** the strong one-bar side of the high-`t` obstruction fork through `t=(q-k)/D<=1.8687`  
**Dependencies:** linear strong-TB1 density; common diameter-routing hub; one-sided interval support; ordered convex diameter-thrackle geometry; one-turn hubward path-region order; source-wide diameter-degree bound  
**Certificate:** `research/verify_target_c_high_t_strong_tb1_support_trichotomy_t18687.py`  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** `STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.`

The strong-TB1 alternative was previously a positive-density family of distinct local blockers with a fixed angular margin, but its laminar support ends were not globally organized. The same common-hub and convex-thrackle order used on the exact-antipodal force rail applies to one chosen strong blocker per interface.

The result is a linear-scale trichotomy. A surviving strong-TB1 branch contains a positive linear family which is either

1. a proper hub-crossing support staircase;
2. a nested rail joining one fixed pre-hub host arc to one fixed hubward host arc; or
3. a nested rail whose bars all die at one post-hub interface, forcing a macroscopic switching separator there.

Every bar in all three alternatives retains the fixed strong-TB1 first-order blocking margin.

---

## 1. Setup

Let `S_D` be the strong-TB1 family from the corrected high-`t` fork. In the strong-TB1 alternative,

\[
|S_D|>s_0D-o(D),
\qquad
s_0:={7c_0\over16}
={791\over320{,}000{,}000}
=2.471875\times10^{-6},
\tag{ST.1}
\]

where

\[
c_0={113\over20{,}000{,}000}.
\tag{ST.2}
\]

For each `i in S_D`, retain the chosen strong switching diameter bar `h_i`. Its consistently oriented diameter vector `n_i` satisfies the uniform tangent-blocking estimate

\[
\boxed{
 n_i\cdot w\ge D\sin\gamma
 \quad\text{for every unit }w\in\Omega_i,
}
\tag{ST.3}
\]

with

\[
\gamma={c_0\over32}
={113\over640{,}000{,}000}>0.
\tag{ST.4}
\]

All selected interfaces lie on one side of the common hub. Assume they are pre-hub; the post-hub case is obtained by reversing the chain. For a selected interface `i<k`, one-sided interval support gives

\[
I_i=\{i+1,\ldots,b_i\},
\qquad b_i\ge k.
\tag{ST.5}
\]

Thus `h_i` has one birth endpoint in the middle layer `R_i` and one partner endpoint on the hubward shore of the common cut `tau_k`.

---

## 2. A linear subfamily has births on one fixed host side

The internal rectangle layer `R_i` meets the outer boundary in two host-side subarcs, denoted `C_i^+` and `C_i^-`. Every switching diameter endpoint in `R_i` lies on one of these two subarcs.

Color `i in S_D` by which host-side subarc contains the birth endpoint of `h_i`. One color class has size

\[
\boxed{
M>{s_0\over2}D-o(D).
}
\tag{ST.6}

Fix that class and write its interface tags in increasing order,

\[
i_1<i_2<\cdots<i_M<k.
\tag{ST.7}
\]

All birth endpoints are distinct. Along the fixed physical host arc the subarcs `C_{i_s}^\sigma` are pairwise disjoint and appear in interface order or its reverse.

Every selected bar crosses the common hub cut `tau_k`. Let `P` be the birth shore and `Q` the hubward partner shore.

---

## 3. The partner map is weakly ordered even when partners collide

Take two retained bars with birth endpoints `x,x' in P` in their boundary order. If their partner endpoints `y,y' in Q` are distinct, then the two diameter edges are vertex-disjoint: their births are distinct and the two shores are disjoint. Actual diameter edges form a convex geometric thrackle, so the chords cross. Hence their partner endpoints occur in the compatible boundary order on `Q`.

If `y=y'`, record a tie.

Therefore the partner map from the ordered births to `Q` is weakly order-preserving. Repeated partner vertices create only equal boundary positions and equal path-region labels.

Along `Q`, the ambient path-region labels have one turn:

\[
R_k,R_{k+1},\ldots,R_m,
\quad R_m,\quad
R_m,R_{m-1},\ldots,R_k.
\tag{ST.8}
\]

Allowing repeated vertices within a region, this is weakly nondecreasing and then weakly nonincreasing. Reversing the boundary order preserves the same class of one-turn words.

Consequently, in the interface order (ST.7), the selected support-end sequence

\[
(b_1,b_2,\ldots,b_M)
\tag{ST.9}
\]

is weakly unimodal after the harmless global orientation choice.

---

## 4. Half of the fixed-side family has monotone support ends

A weakly unimodal sequence has a weakly nondecreasing prefix and a weakly nonincreasing suffix whose union is the whole sequence and which overlap at a maximum plateau. One of these two monotone pieces has length at least `M/2`.

Therefore there is a consecutive-in-interface-order subfamily `L` with

\[
\boxed{
|L|>{s_0\over4}D-o(D)
}
\tag{ST.10}

on which the support ends `b_s` are monotone.

There are two cases.

### Staircase case

If `b_s` is weakly nondecreasing with increasing birth interface, then both support endpoints move hubward:

\[
i_1<i_2<\cdots,
\qquad
b_1\le b_2\le\cdots.
\tag{ST.11}
\]

All intervals still contain the common hub cut. This is a proper hub-crossing support staircase.

### Nested case

If `b_s` is weakly nonincreasing, then

\[
\boxed{
I_1\supsetneq I_2\supsetneq\cdots.
}
\tag{ST.12}
\]

Strictness follows from the strictly increasing birth indices. This gives a nested strong-TB1 rail of size greater than `s_0D/4-o(D)`.

The blocking inequality (ST.3) survives unchanged in either case.

---

## 5. A nested strong-TB1 rail is cross-host or death-concentrated

Assume the nested case. The same one-turn argument used for the linear exact-force rail needs only:

- births on one fixed pre-hub host side;
- weak partner order on `Q`;
- monotone support-end labels; and
- the one-turn region word on `Q`.

It does not use exact antipodality or a two-bar force pair.

Hence the nested rail splits into

\[
L=L_{host}\sqcup L_{plateau},
\tag{ST.13}
\]

where every bar in `L_host` joins the fixed pre-hub birth arc to one fixed hubward host arc, while every bar in `L_plateau` has one common support-end tag `b_*` and therefore belongs to the same post-hub switching set `X_{b_*}`.

Thus

\[
\boxed{
|L_{host}|\ge{|L|\over2}
\quad\text{or}\quad
|L_{plateau}|\ge{|L|\over2}.}
\tag{ST.14}
\]

Combining (ST.10) and (ST.14), one of the three alternatives

```text
STAIRCASE,
CROSS-HOST NESTED,
ONE-DEATH PLATEAU
```

has size

\[
\boxed{
>{s_0\over8}D-o(D)
={791\over2{,}560{,}000{,}000}D-o(D)
=(3.08984375\times10^{-7})D-o(D).
}
\tag{ST.15}

The staircase alternative actually retains the stronger `s_0/4` lower density.

---

## 6. The plateau alternative is again a macroscopic separator

In the one-death alternative, all selected bars are distinct because their birth endpoints lie in distinct layer subarcs. Hence

\[
N_{b_*}=|X_{b_*}|\ge |L_{plateau}|.
\tag{ST.16}
\]

Therefore

\[
\boxed{
N_{b_*}>{s_0\over8}D-o(D).
}
\tag{ST.17}
\]

Since the joint-cost quantity satisfies `J_j>=N_j`, the common death interface obeys

\[
J_{b_*}>{s_0\over8}D-o(D).
\tag{ST.18}
\]

Thus it lies outside every fixed-threshold good class for sufficiently large `D`. The strong-TB1 branch can fail to produce a cross-host or staircase rail only by concentrating linear raw switching complexity at one post-hub separator.

This is a cardinality statement. It gives no KKT multiplier floor at `b_*`.

---

## 7. Endpoint-disjoint crossing extraction preserves the strong angular margin

Birth endpoints in each of the three alternatives are distinct. Only partner collisions need to be removed. The source-wide bound

\[
\Delta(H)\le(4\,3^{1/8}+o(1))D^{3/4}
\tag{ST.19}
\]

implies that greedy selection of at most one chosen bar per partner vertex retains, from any branch satisfying (ST.15),

\[
\boxed{
>
\left({s_0\over32\,3^{1/8}}-o(1)\right)D^{1/4}
}
\tag{ST.20}

endpoint-disjoint bars. Every two retained bars then cross geometrically.

Numerically,

\[
\boxed{
{s_0\over32\,3^{1/8}}
\approx6.73343031652\times10^{-8}.
}
\tag{ST.21}

Every retained bar still obeys the same fixed first-order margin (ST.3). Thus the strong-TB1 branch contains an unbounded pairwise-crossing ordered support rail whose blockers are uniformly interior to their local opening dual cones.

---

## 8. Frontier effect

The positive-density strong-TB1 branch is no longer merely a list of unrelated robust local blockers. It has one of three linear ordered forms:

```text
STAIRCASE:
  birth and death interface tags both advance monotonically
  + every support contains the common hub
  + fixed strong-TB1 angular margin

CROSS-HOST NESTED:
  strictly nested supports
  + one fixed pre-hub birth host arc
  + one fixed hubward partner host arc
  + fixed strong-TB1 angular margin

ONE-DEATH PLATEAU:
  strictly nested supports
  + one common death interface b_*
  + N_{b_*}=Theta(D) raw switching complexity
  + fixed strong-TB1 angular margin at the birth interfaces.
```

This puts the strong-TB1 side into the same ordered-support framework as the exact-antipodal side, without assuming exact force cancellation or bilateral force pairs.

The remaining closure work is now branch-specific. The staircase and cross-host branches should be compared with the common inverse-`D` near-triangular train grid and near-central diameter geometry. The plateau branch should be attacked at its single macroscopic post-hub separator using the exact cut resultant, terminal ledger, and direction/separation budgets.

No C-II or Level-I closure is claimed.

**Disposition:** converted a positive-density strong-TB1 family into a linear ordered-support trichotomy while preserving its fixed first-order blocking margin. C-II and Level I remain open.
